perates through two segments, FEI-NY and FEI-Zyfer. The FEI-NY segment offers precision time and frequency control products for communication satellites, terrestrial cellular telephone or other ground-based telecommunication stations; and other components and systems for the U.S. military, as well as provides designing and technical support for the satellite business. The FEI-Zyfer segment provides global positioning system technologies for systems and subsystems for secure communications, government and commercial, and other locator applications; and engages in the sale and support of wireline telecommunications products, such as US5G. It operates in the United States, Europe, and Asia. Frequency Electronics, Inc. was founded in 1961 and is headquartered in Mitchel Field, New York.","ceo_bio":"Dr. Thomas  McClelland | CEO, President & Director | Age 70 | Compensation: $630,827 (FY2026)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 232 people. Revenue per employee of $273K ranks in the below the sector median among Technology peers. Stock-based compensation of $9K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 2\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (6/10).","financial_summary":["Frequency Electronics, Inc. is a small-cap technology business with a $719M market cap on $63M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +16% over the past three years and +0% annually across the full decade, the kind of sustained demand persistence that marks a compounder.","Returns on capital run at -3% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests, with gross margins sitting at 31%.","The company is burning through $2M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","On the balance sheet, thin -34.5\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.2%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 12% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Operating margins 12% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Carries a 440% premium to sector median EV/EBITDA","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":4.402923872348012},{"text":"Elevated short interest (10.8% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting the other way","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Heavy insider selling (net $37.5M)","sev":"amber","cat":"insider","dollars":-37500005.0},{"text":"Financial quality metrics lag Technology peers","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Technology sector lagging the market significantly, underperforming by 7% over 3 months","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Elevated sector volatility (38% annualized) adds uncertainty","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Cybersecurity liability and talent cost inflation \u2014 a single breach can erase years of goodwill, and the war for engineering talent compresses margins even at scale.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Altman Z comfortably in the safe zone \u2014 a fortress balance sheet on this measure","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Contrasting B.O.S. Better Online Solutions (NASDAQ:BOSC) and Frequency Electronics (NASDAQ:FEIM)","source":"thelincolnianonline.com","link":"https://www.thelincolnianonline.com/2026/11/11/contrasting-b-o-s-better-online-soluti